---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const log = [
  ['Harvested the source', 'The MyHeritage tree read in full — 164 people, 648 facts with their citations. Five duplicate records found and collapsed; about a hundred people identified as a surname index rather than descent.', 'doc'],
  ['Checked it against the Bundesarchiv', 'All four Leibholz men found in the Gedenkbuch. Jacob was deported on 26 February 1943, not killed in 1945 — and Georg, born at Hammerstein two months after Jacob was born at Schubin, cannot be his brother.', 'doc'],
  ['Read the DNA', 'Derrick carries 0.0% Ashkenazi Jewish where a quarter is required, and 1.4% Germanic where a quarter is required. The paternity of record does not survive it.', 'inf'],
  ['Found her name', 'The 1948 divorce file names her in full: Ivone, born MOTA FIGUEIREDO. "Mota" was never a nickname. Her father was a Figueiredo and her mother a Mota.', 'doc'],
  ['Surveyed Mozambique', 'FamilySearch has never filmed anything genealogical for Mozambique. The Arquivo Histórico de Moçambique holds the civil registers for 1865–1934 and takes requests online — and Ivone was born in 1924.', 'doc'],
  ['Worked the passport registers', 'Porto closed as a dead end: its series begins in 1951. Aveiro gave one candidate, Augusto Pereira de Figueiredo, bound for Lourenço Marques in October 1904.', 'doc'],
  ['Read the 1883 baptism book', 'Fifty-one images, no name index, entry Nº 35. Augusto born 12 August 1883, four grandparents named — and a marginal note giving his death at Amadora in 1954, with the registration number that will decide whether he is Ivone’s father.', 'doc'],
  ['Worked the British DNA cluster', 'Five matches who are first cousins to each other are all second cousins to Derrick. One sits a generation closer — her grandparent was a sibling of his biological father, and her own father was born in Johannesburg. Surnames: Halsey, Parr, Fenton.', 'inf'],
  ['Yad Vashem', 'Samuel appears in the List of Jews murdered in Auschwitz as Samuel ISRAEL Leibholz — which is where the compulsory Nazi middle name on this family’s records comes from. Oskar confirmed on the Theresienstadt list, Georg on the Łódź list.', 'doc'],
  ['Opened the photographs', 'Twenty-two people in the tree carry attached images, and several of the “photographs” are documents nobody had read: Julius Leibke’s 1942 Berlin death register, naming a whole new generation — Abraham Leibke and Christine née Berger — and Henriette Katz’s 1926 newspaper death notice, in which the family itself pairs Trude with Oskar.', 'doc'],
  ['Read the 1956 marriage register', 'The largest of those images, 3,918 × 5,245 px, turned out to be the duplicate original register of Kurt’s second marriage — entry No. 169 of 1956. It records his status as DIVORCEE with an order filed, which confirms the 1951 decree from outside the court; and its parents’ columns, which this form has and the 1948 form did not, are ruled through and empty.', 'doc'],
  ['Wrote to three archives', 'Letters sent 15 September 2026, in Portuguese, to the Arquivo da Defesa Nacional at Paço d\'Arcos, the Arquivo Histórico Militar in Lisbon and the Arquivo Histórico de Moçambique in Maputo, asking whether Henrique was recenseado at Lourenço Marques in 1961 and what his military registration says. The Maputo letter is a different letter: this archive already knew their civil registers stop at 1934, so it does not ask for a birth certificate — it asks whether the recruitment-district records stayed in Mozambique after 1975. Each archive is told what the others have been asked.', 'doc'],
  ['WITHDRAWN: the estate notice that was not Kurt\'s', 'On 14 September this archive announced that it had found Kurt\'s estate notice in Government Gazette 2525 of 26 September 1969. On 15 September the gazette was downloaded and read in full. The word Luwinski does not appear in it once. The estate number 7584/69 on that page belongs to KOTZE, Pieter Johannes of Bethal, and the Sanlam Building address quoted with it belongs to the notice immediately above, ROBBERTZE, Alida Christina. Two adjacent entries in a two-column list, and the archive took the number from one and the address from the other. The notice is not found. Kurt\'s estate is known from the National Archives index and nothing else.', 'disp'],
  ['Found Henrique\'s estate', 'The same free gazette archive that found the hat shop also found the deceased estate of Ivone\'s first son — number 12294/2023, gazetted three times in 2023 as DA ROCHA, HENRIQUE FIGUEIREDO PINTO. A South African estate file contains form J294, which asks for the father\'s full names. The man the family tree can only call "Unknown da Rocha" is named on a printed form in a government office, and the file can be ordered by number.', 'doc'],
  ['Found the shop', 'A page of the Union Government Gazette for 30 October 1942, supplied by the family, names Kurt in a partnership notice. Chasing it found Gazettes.Africa — free, full-text, 54,313 South African issues — and eight Luwinski entries. He bought the Piccadilly Hat Company at 81 Eloff Street on 1 November 1941, ran it with Walter Oppenheimer for exactly a year, took it over alone on 31 October 1942, and was still trading there in 1952. Fifteen blank years between his arrival and his marriage are no longer blank.', 'doc'],
  ['Swept all 5,464 matches by surname', 'The archive\'s oldest outstanding item, closed. The search box on the DNA Matches page takes a name OR an ancestral surname and searches the whole list. LEIBHOLZ: no matches. LUWINSKI: no matches. MyHeritage indexes both as Derrick\'s own ancestral surnames, so the search had real terms and still returned nothing across 5,464 people. The German and Jewish absence is now an exhaustive search rather than a sample of six.', 'doc'],
  ['Withdrew the Portuguese half of it, hours later', 'The same sweep returned 27 for FIGUEIREDO and 13 for MOTA, and this archive called it the best lead on Ivone\'s parentage it had ever had — before opening a single match. Opened, the Figueiredo tag turns out to pair her with a woman born before 1713, and MyHeritage states that the Mota matches share no ancestral surname with Derrick at all. A surname tag on a match card is a string comparison between two trees, not a finding. Withdrawn in public, because that is what the corrections layer is for.', 'disp'],
  ['Tested the Y-branch surnames', 'FamilyTreeDNA Discover names the surname projects on R-S11477 — Ballew, Stafford, Blakely, Byrd, York. All five were searched against Derrick\'s match list. Everything came back between 10 and 28 cM, fourth-to-fifth cousin, the noise floor — exactly what a common ancestor in 200 BCE predicts. A negative worth recording, because it says which tool to reach for next and it is the autosomal one.', 'inf'],
  ['The Y-DNA came back', 'R-S11477 — R1b, branch U106/Z381, the North Sea lineage that peaks in the Netherlands and England, and not one characteristic of Ashkenazi paternal descent. The archive had named this as the test that would break the paternity finding or confirm it. It confirmed it, from an independent direction, and the label stays Inferred because a conclusion does not become Documented by surviving a test.', 'doc'],
  ['The 1939 census', 'Addresses at last. Jacob, Auguste and Siegfried at Meraner Straße 2, Schöneberg. And two corrections: Gertrud Leibke was Oskar’s wife, not Georg’s — and Georg’s wife, Lydia née Perl, died on the same day he did.', 'doc'],
  ['Read two words in column four', 'The 1952 Johannesburg marriage register was opened at full resolution to look for the parents of Mervin Halsey. The 1952 form has no parents\' columns — but it has column (4), Country of Birth, and it says Mervin was born in the CAPE PROVINCE and his bride Cynthia Wear in ENGLAND. A week spent on the surname Halsey had been spent on the wrong half of the couple. Cynthia\'s GRO birth entry (Q2 1931, Hitchin, 3A/1345) names her mother as a CASTLE; the only Wear-Castle marriage in the entire English index is Hitchin, Q4 1914; the 1921 census gives her as Martha Maud Wear, born Baldock, Hertfordshire; and the 1901 and 1911 censuses put the whole Castle household at Baldock — with two children, Hilda b.1902 and Ernest b.1904, that the match\'s own tree never recorded. Two of Derrick\'s closest matches descend from that one couple through two different daughters, 297.5 and 292.7 cM. The Castles were never Norfolk; only the Browns were.', 'doc'],
  ['Cross-matched the cluster, then read the segments', 'Centimorgan totals could not separate two readings of where Derrick sat. The shared SEGMENTS could, because they answer a different question — not how much he shares with each match but which parts of his own genome. Of the 297.5 cM he shares with the closest match, only about 44 fall in regions he also shares with any Castle cousin: one block on chromosome 2. The other 253 lie nowhere near them. Two people who share a single ancestral couple cannot do that. He and she descend from Henry Robert Wear AND Martha Maud Castle together — a couple, not a line — so his father was their son.', 'doc'],
  ['Found the man', 'LEONARD AUBREY ROBERT WEAR, birth registered Hitchin 1917, on the 1921 census at 72 Pix Road, Letchworth, aged four — and then absent from every English record there is. He was in Johannesburg: married Phyllis Agnes Robertson at Rosebank on 7 September 1940, divorced by her in 1951, married again that October, divorced again in 1962. Thirty-one in June 1948, in the right city, married to somebody else. And he is why his sister Cynthia was in Johannesburg to marry there in 1952.', 'inf'],
  ['Closed the sibship', 'The GRO birth index carries the mother\u2019s maiden name from 1911, so the whole family can be listed rather than guessed at. Surname WEAR, mother\u2019s maiden name CASTLE, all of England and Wales: six children, every one at Hitchin — 1917, 1919, 1921, 1931, 1933, 1936 — of whom two were sons. In June 1948 one was thirty-one and the other was eleven.', 'doc'],
  ['Then the whole family turned up in Johannesburg', 'Henry Robert Wear died there on 11 September 1969, aged 80, and is buried at West Park; Martha Maud died there on 16 March 1986, aged 90; Bruce Wallace married at Kuruman in 1959. The household this archive had reconstructed in a Hertfordshire market town had picked itself up and moved to the city Derrick was born in.', 'doc'],
  ['Somebody else had joined him to a Castle in 2018', 'A seven-person MyHeritage tree, kept by a South African family who know nothing of Baldock or Letchworth, gives Leonard Wear b.1917 UK d.1973 a mother called \u201cMuad Wear, born Castle\u201d. Muad is Maud. No record anywhere else gives him parents — FamilySearch\u2019s own profile has none to this day. And its profile page labels him \u201cFather (of site manager)\u201d, which identifies the masked daughter as the woman who built it.', 'doc'],
  ['Swept a source that indexes women by maiden name', 'gendatabase.com, given by the family. It answered in four seconds a question that had taken a day: EDWINA ANN WEAR, the candidate half-sister, was born EDWINA ANN GREEN and married Bruce Wallace Wear — Leonard\u2019s younger brother — in 1980. A Wear by marriage, no blood relation at all. Every dead end around her had been the answer telling us so. It also produced three Luwinski court files nobody had seen: WLD 1950/1948, the divorce Kurt filed against Ivone within months of marrying her, and WLD 497/1951 and 2368/1951 behind it.', 'doc'],
  ['Rebuilt four cemetery pages a tile at a time, and answered the 1986 question with a no', 'The West Park deep-zoom images were reassembled from their tiles \u2014 377 for the 1986 spread alone \u2014 and magnified to five times. The result: the register DOES NOT NAME who authorised reopening grave 56045 in 1986. Martha Maud\u2019s Remarks column is empty, and the index side of the private-graves folio holds exactly one Wear, her husband. A second interment into an existing private grave generated no grave transaction, so nobody signed for it. The record was never made, which is a different answer from not having found it, and the row closes with the reason attached.', 'inf'],
  ['CORRECTED: the grave folio is W 8, not W58', 'Published on 15 September as folio W58. The pencilled head-margin number, re-cropped and read on its own at 4.3 times, is a looped W and a single 8 \u2014 the flourish on the W had been read as a 5. Three records agree: the purchaser page\u2019s own margin, the index side of the same folio, and the burial register\u2019s cross-reference \u201cP. Fol. W.8.\u201d Nothing else in the entry changes \u2014 not the purchaser, not the address, not the sixty pounds. But a folio number is how the next person orders the page, and published wrong it sends them to the wrong sheet.', 'disp'],
  ['Got the parents\u2019 own address, and demoted a guess', 'Burial register folio W 56 has an address column, and row 4 is Henry Robert Wear: died 11 September 1969 of bronchopneumonia, aged 80, at the General Hospital, Johannesburg; usual residence 5 LIVINGSTONE CT., 4TH ST., PARKHURST. The first address ever documented for either parent in South Africa. It also bracketed an inference this archive had been leaning on: 64 Greenfield Road, Greenside was never documented as their house \u2014 it was reasoned from an unmarried daughter of twenty-one. The parents are now documented in Parkhurst in 1969 and the widow still in Parkhurst in 1986. Greenside survives as a guess, and now says so.', 'doc'],
  ['Read a dead hostname as a dead service, and was wrong', 'On 16 September the National Archives search system stopped resolving and this archive published that NAAIRS was down. The hostname really had died. THE SERVICE HAD NOT — it had moved to www.national.archsrch.gov.za under a new path, and the portal page the family opened gave the new address in one click. A paragraph telling the Reading Room supervisor that her own system was down was cut out of a letter minutes before it was sent. A URL that does not resolve is a fact about a URL.', 'disp'],
  ['Published two zeroes that were not zeroes', 'The spelling sweep reported LAWINSKI and LUBINSKI as returning nothing in the archives index. Both return three records each. The page now carries the correction in place rather than a silent edit, because NOTHING FOUND and FOUND, AND NOT OURS are different claims and only one of them is a negative.', 'disp'],
  ['Claimed DNA above George William Castle, and withdrew it inside the hour', 'Two match holders carry the surname GIDDINS, 43.36 and 62.89 cM, both in cluster 12 — and James Giddins of Norton End had just been proved as Derrick\'s three-times-great-grandfather. The arithmetic fitted a fourth cousin exactly. Three paragraphs were written calling it the first DNA to touch the Castle line above George William. THEN THE TREE WAS OPENED. Its George Giddins was born on 17 July 1846 at WITHYBROOK, WARWICKSHIRE, father Richard — a different family in a different county. The matches are real and the surname is not the reason for them. The arithmetic was sound and the premise was never checked, and checking it took four minutes.', 'disp'],
  ['Settled WALLIS against WALLS, six to nothing', 'This archive had used both spellings and asserted neither, which is a contradiction to leave lying on a page for weeks. The GRO birth index can be searched on the MOTHER\'S maiden name: Castle births at Hitchin return SIX children under WALLIS — 1884, 1887, 1889, 1892, 1901, 1904 — and none at all under Walls. That is a registrar writing her maiden name down six times across twenty years, against one census in which an enumerator heard her widowed father as Joseph Walls. Walls is withdrawn.', 'doc'],
  ['Reversed the emigration and had it backwards in public first', 'The published account had the father go out first and the son follow. LEONARD SAILED ON 12 MARCH 1937, two years before either parent — a man of twenty going alone, not a family sending for its children. Henry Robert followed on 5 April 1939 and Martha Maud on 10 August with the four youngest, three weeks before the declaration of war.', 'disp'],
  ['Leaked three living match holders into a build, and found out from a guard that was not running', 'Two named living people reached the published pages and a third reached the work list. The check that exists precisely to stop this — it compares every published page against the private match list — HAD NEVER BEEN WIRED INTO THE BUILD CHAIN. It is now, and it runs on every build: 111 match holders checked against every page. All three names were stripped. A guard that is written and not called is not a guard.', 'disp'],
  ['CORRECTED: Martha Giddings had her son for something under a year', 'This archive wrote, and put in a letter to a living second cousin, that if Martha Giddings died in 1863 she could not have known her son at all. SHE DIED IN THE THIRD QUARTER of 1863; GEORGE WILLIAM WAS BAPTISED ON 4 JANUARY of it. Two numbers, both already on file, and the quarters were never compared. It changes the reading of the 1871 census as well: the widower is IN the Giddins house at Norton End, enumerated as son-in-law with his boy as grandson, so he did not hand the child over — he went to his dead wife\'s parents and stayed eight years. A correction has been drafted to the cousin who was told the wrong thing.', 'disp'],
  ['Found Caroline where nobody could follow her, because she changed her name', 'Every tree loses Caroline Wallis after her husband died in 1916. She remarried in Q3 1920 at Hitchin — CAROLINE CASTLE to JAMES JAMES — and was CAROLINE JAMES for the last thirty-seven years of her life, which is why her death in 1957 could not be found under either of the surnames people were searching. The 1939 Register has her at 38 Norton Road, Baldock, widowed a second time, and gives her exact date of birth: 28 FEBRUARY 1863, not the 1862 every secondary source repeats.', 'doc'],
  ['The transport has a number, and the memorial book was not the one we thought', 'Yad Vashem had been used here for exactly one list. Searched properly, LEIBHOLZ returns 88 victim records across four collections — and Jacob\'s record carries a field this archive never had: "Details of Transport: TRANSPORT 30 from Berlin to Auschwitz Birkenau, Extermination Camp, Poland on 26/02/1943." The date has been on these pages for a fortnight. THE TRAIN HAD NOT. It also gives his citizenship as GERMANY, which matters to the statelessness argument two pages away. OSKAR\'S RECORD closes the distance between his two deportations: Berlin to Theresienstadt 17 March 1943, Theresienstadt to Auschwitz 16 October 1944, and DATE OF DEATH 16 OCTOBER 1944 — the same day the second transport left. AND THE SOURCE BEHIND THE TRANSPORT NUMBER IS A BOOK THIS ARCHIVE DID NOT KNOW IT WAS STANDING ON: not the Bundesarchiv Gedenkbuch cited on every page here, but the GEDENKBUCH BERLINS DER JÜDISCHEN OPFER DES NATIONALSOZIALISMUS, Freie Universität Berlin, Edition Hentrich, 1995 — Berlin\'s own. It has never been consulted directly and it should be. One discrepancy settles against Arolsen and in the Bundesarchiv\'s favour: Yad Vashem has GEORG born 1884, not 1885. And one decoy is written down before it costs anybody a day — "KURT LEIBHOLZ, 1927, Berlin, murdered" IS NOT OUR KURT, who was a Luwinski, born 1912, and died in Johannesburg in 1968.', 'doc'],
  ['Three of them are on one document, and one of the three had been gone ten years', 'The Arolsen Archives had sat on the sources page under "identified, not yet worked" since 14 September. Worked on the 17th. The index needs no consent from anybody and it holds 213 Leibholz entries. It confirms JAKOB 08.08.1884 SCHUBIN and OSKAR 13.05.1887 SCHUBIN from records with no connection to the Bundesarchiv — and only those two Leibholzes in the whole index are recorded as born at Schubin. It carries two of the brothers under the compulsory name, LEIBHOLZ ISRAEL OSKAR and LEIBHOLZ GEORG ISRAEL, which puts the 1938 decree published here this morning onto the family\'s own paper. It holds a post-war tracing case for Oskar, T/D 52.971, fourteen documents, which exists because somebody asked. AND THEN A SEARCH FOR AUGUSTE RETURNED SOMEBODY THIS ARCHIVE DID NOT KNOW WAS IN THE PERSECUTION RECORDS AT ALL: "LEIBHOLZ, Auguste, maiden name LUWWINSKY, born 28.08.1893". That is Lizzy, carried here for a fortnight as 1893-1975 with no date — and the maiden name is spelt with a DOUBLED W, a seventh form of the surname and the first that is German rather than South African. THEN THE DOCUMENT IDENTIFIERS WERE COMPARED. Jacob, Auguste and Kurt ALL RESOLVE TO DocID 11214358, folder MFN01335, under Deportations and Transports. IT IS ONE DOCUMENT. The father who was deported, the mother who was not, and the son who had been in Johannesburg for ten years, all three named on a single sheet. This morning it was written here that Kurt was probably "named inside a document about somebody else"; by the afternoon the somebody else had a name and it was his father. WHAT STAYS LABELLED: the shared DocID is a hard fact from the index; what kind of document it is, is not, because nobody here has read it — opening one requires ticking a declaration of consent about the use of sensitive personal data, and this archive does not tick that on anybody\'s behalf.', 'doc'],
  ['A surname leaving a city, and a control that proves it is not the scanner', 'David asked what more could be done on the Leibholz and Luwinski side. THE WHOLE LINE LIVED IN BERLIN AND THIS ARCHIVE HAD NEVER LOOKED IN THE BERLIN DIRECTORY. The Zentral- und Landesbibliothek has digitised the Berliner Adressbücher from 1799 to 1970 — one volume a year, six to seven thousand pages each, householders by NAME, TRADE AND STREET — free, no account, answering a JSON query. 1,047 page hits for LEIBHOLZ, 23 for LUWINSKI. THE FIRST THING OUT OF IT: Leibholz page hits per annual volume run 24 in 1933 and hold between 15 and 24 through the thirties — then 12 in 1940, FIVE in 1941, six in 1942, TWO IN 1943. AND THE CONTROL IS NOT AN IDLE ONE. THIMM — Johanna Thimm of Sauerbaum is Kurt\'s own great-grandmother, East Prussian and not Jewish — runs 66, 53, 47, 65, 72, 68, 45, 75, 69, 63 AND 75 IN 1943: as common in the last volume as in the first. The scanner did not get worse, the Fraktur did not get harder, the book did not get smaller. THE LIMITS GO IN WITH IT AND THEY MATTER MORE THAN THE NUMBER: a page hit is a page and not a person, twenty-four to two is a small base, and DEPORTATION, EMIGRATION AND ADMINISTRATIVE EXCLUSION FROM DIRECTORIES ALL LOOK IDENTICAL IN A COUNT — Jews were being struck out of German commercial listings from 1938 quite apart from being deported, and a count cannot tell those apart. AND A LEAD: the Luwinskis of Berlin are in the garment trade. ALFRED LUWINSKI, BÜGLER — a presser — at Putbusser Straße 24 in Wedding in 1933 and 1936, a lorry driver by 1943, while Lizzy was a dressmaker and milliner in the same city. A brother is the obvious guess and it is a guess.', 'doc'],
  ['Two hundred and nineteen commits citing Kew, and the catalogue had never been opened', 'David asked whether this archive had ever looked at The National Archives. IT HAD NOT. The pages carry RG10, RG11, RG12, RG13, RG14, RG15, BT 27, WO 329, WO 372 and WO 392 — hundreds of references to records held at Kew — and every one came second-hand through a commercial index\'s transcript. Discovery is free, needs no account and answers JSON. The first hour produced four things. EVERY CITATION VERIFIES, and two verify BETTER than they were published: WO 392/15 is specifically "Section 5: South Africa (Union Defence Forces)", which is exactly the claim this archive makes about Leonard Wear, and WO 329/1120 adds the page range 312-622. WO 372/4/50790 IS THE EXACT ITEM REFERENCE for Fred James Castle\'s medal card, in Kew\'s own words — "Scottish Rifles 10061 Serjeant. Scottish Rifles 7815432 Serjeant. Machine Gun Corps 195568 Serjeant." Findmypast gave only the piece and PRINTED THE DISCOVERY ID ON ITS OWN TRANSCRIPT; this archive had it for hours and never followed it. DP/12/1/14 AND DP/12/1/15 ARE THE BALDOCK BAPTISM REGISTERS, 1880-1895 and 1895-1910, at Hertfordshire Archives — the register this archive had been gesturing at all day, now an orderable reference. And LUWINSKI RETURNS ZERO across 2,500+ British archives, filed with the reason a catalogue is not a name index. A reusable tool went in with it, tools/tna.py, because the failure here was not ignorance of the source but never having reached for it.', 'doc'],
  ['Found him: Sergeant 10061, Cameronians (Scottish Rifles), and a career soldier into the 1920s', 'FREDERICK JAMES CASTLE is the only Castle male line this archive has and he had been missing from every English record since 1901. He is found, and the chain runs: the 1901 census has him at eleven at NORTON END, BALDOCK, son of George W and Caroline; the 1911 census has a Frederick Castle, 21, single, born Herts Baldock, in MEEANEE BARRACKS, COLCHESTER — and the ORIGINAL IMAGE, read at 299 per cent, gives column 5, UNIT OR ARM OF SERVICE, as SCOTTISH RIFLES, which the transcript leaves blank; and the campaign medal roll, TNA WO 329/1120, gives FRED JAMES CASTLE, SERGEANT, 10061, CAMERONIANS (SCOTTISH RIFLES), British War Medal and Victory Medal. THIS MORNING THE IDENTIFICATION RESTED ON ONE CENSUS AGE. It now rests on two matches arrived at from opposite ends — the forenames FRED JAMES, exactly as the General Register Office registered him in 1889, and the regiment, exactly as an enumerator wrote it over his line in 1911. AND HIS MEDAL INDEX CARD, WO 372/4, EXPLAINS EVERY ABSENCE THE ROW WAS BUILT ON: Sergeant throughout, THREE service numbers — 10061 Scottish Rifles, 195568 MACHINE GUN CORPS, and 7815432 Scottish Rifles. An eight-digit number is a post-1920 issue. HE WAS STILL A SERGEANT AFTER THE WAR, which is why he is in no 1921 household, no 1939 Register and no English death index. Not proved, and the file says so: the medal roll carries no birth date and no birthplace, so the link is forenames plus regiment and not a document stating identity. A service record would state it, because it names next of kin.', 'inf'],
  ['Nine born, eight living, one died — and all nine are now named', 'The 1911 census asks a married woman how many children she has borne. Caroline answered NINE. This archive could name seven, and the baptism register turned out to stop in 1879. It closed anyway, from the household. THE 1901 CENSUS HAS THE FAMILY WHOLE — RG13/1298 folio 49 page 26, schedule 192, NORTON END, NORTON, BALDOCK: George W Castle 36, Caroline 36 born WESTON, and five children including FREDERICK, 11, and MARTHA, 5. THEY HAD GONE BACK TO NORTON END, the hamlet where George William was himself raised by his GIDDINS grandparents after his mother died; he brought his own family home to it. THE NINTH CHILD IS SARAH ANN WALLIS, born 19 August 1882 at Weston, TWO MONTHS BEFORE HER PARENTS MARRIED AND REGISTERED UNDER HER MOTHER\'S MAIDEN NAME — not a Castle birth in the index at all, which is why no search for Castle births could have found her. AND THE ONE WHO DIED IS NAMED: SIDNEY CHARLES CASTLE, born Q2 1898 at Hitchin, DIED Q4 1900 AT HITCHIN, AGED TWO. One entry in each index, no competitors, and he was gone five months before the 1901 census, which is why no census ever showed him. The arithmetic closes exactly. AND THE SIX-INSTEAD-OF-NINE WAS THREE SEPARATE INDEXING FAILURES: Sarah Ann filed under Wallis, Martha Maud with a blank mother\'s maiden name, Sidney Charles missing too. A filter on a field that is sometimes empty is a filter on the indexing, not on the family.', 'doc'],
  ['Frederick Castle was a soldier, which is why nobody could find him at home', 'He is in the 1901 census after all, aged 11 at Norton End — the earlier search that came back wrong had found the OTHER Norton End Castle family, three schedules away on the same night. And in 1911 there is a BALDOCK-BORN FREDERICK CASTLE, 21, SINGLE, AT MEEANEE BARRACKS, COLCHESTER: RG14 piece 10304, schedule 9999, Military Road and Mersea Road. A regular soldier is not in anybody\'s household. The age is the discriminator and it is thin but real — 21 on 2 April 1911 means born between April 1889 and April 1890, which fits ours and excludes both of the other Baldock Freds. IT IS NOT PROOF and it is labelled probable: the entry says Frederick, not Frederick James, and it rests on one age and a birthplace. What it changes is the shape of the question. The 1921 census holds ONE Baldock-born Frederick Castle in all England and Wales and it is the other family\'s; ours is out of the country by 1921, is not in Soldiers Died, and is in no death index. A regular soldier who vanishes like that went abroad and stayed, or died in a service Soldiers Died does not cover.', 'inf'],
  ['Part of this morning\'s withdrawal comes back, in a different record set', 'The electoral registers were withdrawn today because the set tested — 1832 to 1932 — returns page images and indexes no elector at all. There is a SECOND AND DIFFERENT SET, ENGLAND & WALES ELECTORAL REGISTERS 1910-1932, AND IT CARRIES NAMES: a search for Castle in Hertfordshire returns Arthur Frederick Castle in 1929 and 1930, Frederick Castle in 1919 and 1920. It cannot do what the withdrawn row wanted, because it starts in 1910 and the question was Bermondsey 1885 to 1900. It can place the Baldock Castles year by year from 1919 — the decade in which Caroline became Caroline James and Frederick disappeared. The withdrawal stands; what was wrong was the claim about one set, not the idea.', 'doc'],
  ['Koszalin answered, and the record that would settle Georg Leibholz does not exist', 'The first of eleven institutional letters to come back. Reference O-S.6342.406.2026, signed by the Head of the Slupsk Branch: the archive holds NO registers of the German civil registry office at CZARNE — Standesamt HAMMERSTEIN, Kreis Schlochau — for 1874 to 1899, and NO books of the Jewish community there at all, and NO information about what became of either or where they might now be. That last clause is what makes it a good letter rather than a brush-off. Hammerstein is where GEORG LEIBHOLZ was born on 7 June 1884, and an 1884 birth entry is the one document that would name his parents and settle whether he belongs to this family. This archive holds that he does not — Jacob was born at SCHUBIN in Posen on 8 August 1884, two months later and in another province — and that finding now rests on the Gedenkbuch alone, because the record that could overturn it is gone. SALLY LEIBHOLZ, born 1847, predates civil registration entirely, so only the synagogue books could have held him, and those are the ones that do not exist. Acknowledged the same day, in Polish, in their own thread.', 'doc'],
  ['The baptism register ends five years before the family starts', 'The 1911 census has Caroline answering NINE CHILDREN BORN, EIGHT LIVING, ONE DIED, and this archive can name seven. The baptism register names both parents on every line, so it should have named all nine. ALL 823 CASTLE BAPTISMS IN THE HERTFORDSHIRE SET WERE SORTED BY MOTHER\'S FIRST NAME AND THE CAROLINE BLOCK READ: between the Barbara block and the Catharine block there is EXACTLY ONE ROW. The reason is a coverage bound and not a search failure — sorted by year, the latest of 195 Castle baptisms at Baldock is 1879, and the children were born 1884 to 1904. Martha Maud\'s own Baldock baptism of September 1895 is not in the set either. THE INSTRUMENT ENDS FIVE YEARS BEFORE THE FAMILY STARTS, the two unnamed children stay unnamed, and the answer is in the parish register at Hertfordshire Archives rather than in anything searchable from here. THE ONE ROW IS WORTH HAVING: ADA MAY CASTLE, BAPTISED 10 APRIL 1887 AT WESTON, father GEORGE WILLIAM CASTLE, LABOURER, mother CAROLINE. The first baptism read for any of Caroline\'s children, and it puts the family at Weston — her own birthplace, and where Sarah Ann Wallis was born two months before the marriage.', 'doc'],
  ['Ada May did not die a Castle, and a second woman goes missing the same way', 'The harvested match-holder\'s tree records "Ada May Castle, died Jul 1969, Hitchin". SHE MARRIED IN Q4 1909 AT HITCHIN, volume 3A page 1171, aged twenty-two. And the death index has no such entry: Ada-anything Castle born 1884 to 1890, all England and Wales, returns sixteen rows, NOT ONE at Hitchin and NOT ONE in 1969. A woman who married in 1909 did not die a Castle sixty years later. This is the CAROLINE JAMES trap in miniature, and it is the second time in two days the same mechanism has hidden a woman from this archive — a tree recording a death under a maiden name its owner had long stopped using. Her married surname is NOT guessed here: MarriageFinder offers two page-mates from the index and page-mates are not a marriage. The general lesson is worth more than Ada May — every married daughter in that tree with a death recorded under CASTLE is now suspect.', 'disp'],
  ['Called a result count a source, for the third time in a week', 'Yesterday this archive filed a row saying the England and Wales Electoral Registers had returned NINE ENTRIES FOR WILLIAM WEAR, Surrey and London, 1885 to 1900, and called them the best unworked source for the Bermondsey Wears — an ANNUAL address record covering the years that bracket both brothers\' deaths. TODAY ONE WAS OPENED. Every row in that record set says "NO NAME ON RECORD" and carries a PDF icon: they are page images of volumes, not entries about people. Searched without a place filter, WILLIAM WEAR returns 3,478 of them in DEVON and DURHAM — Weare in Devon, the River Wear in Durham. JOHN SMITH returns the same nameless rows in Lancashire. LUWINSKI returns zero, and so does a nonsense string, so the fields are not inert; they simply match a place. The "Surrey and London" came from a place filter typed by the same hand that then reported it as a finding. A RESULT COUNT IS A PROPERTY OF A SEARCH BOX. This archive has now made that mistake over two Giddins DNA matches, over two archives-index zeroes that were three records each, and here — and it wrote the warning after the first one. WHAT SURVIVES: the Bermondsey volumes are digitised and can be READ page by page for 1885 to 1900. It is an afternoon of images, not a search, and the row now says so.', 'disp'],
  ['TWENTY-ONE EVIDENCE FILES AND NOT ONE BUILD SCRIPT READ ANY OF THEM', 'Asked to check for a fault the sister archive at Mazza had just been caught by — a per-person builder that never opened its own evidence files, so twelve documented people were told on their own pages that nothing had been read about them. This archive has no per-person pages, so it could not fail in that exact shape. IT FAILED IN A WORSE ONE. Every file in data/ was audited against every build script: build-register.py reads the MyHeritage export and corrections.json, the two living-persons guards read the export and a hash file, and THAT IS THE WHOLE LIST. All twenty-one .tsv evidence files — the Gedenkbuch, six censuses, the 1939 Register, the passenger lists, the court files, the Bermondsey households — were read by NOTHING. Their contents reached the site only where a human had retyped them into a paragraph. The clearest casualty: the register printed Samuel Israel Leibholz\'s birth as "June 28 1877?", the source tree\'s question mark intact, while data/leibholz-gedenkbuch.tsv held Bundesarchiv entry en909753 giving 28 June 1877 at Schubin and his death at Auschwitz on 10 April 1943. The archive had the citation and the page showed the doubt. Eleven people — three Blooms of Yarmouth, four Hawkinses of Watford, two Castles, Gertrude Wear, a Letchworth lodger — were documented in the repository and appeared NOWHERE on the published site at all.', 'disp'],
  ['Built the evidence in, and then a gate so it cannot fall out again', 'tools/build-evidence.py now reads every evidence file through an EXPLICIT MANIFEST and writes what each record says about each person; /evidence/ publishes all 135 records about 100 people; and the register carries a RECORD READ column. tools/check-evidence.py runs on every build and refuses it three ways: a file in data/ the manifest does not declare, a stale build, or a person named in an evidence file reaching the published site with neither a record nor a correction. All four failure modes were reintroduced deliberately and all four exit 1. AND IT CHANGES NO DATE. The register and the charts still draw the export as given — the question mark on Samuel\'s birth is still there, because that is what the source says. The record now sits beside it instead of in a file nobody reads. Where a named source genuinely overrides the family record that is a correction, carried as was and now, and it is a different mechanism on purpose.', 'doc'],
  ['The new builder hung three divorce actions on the wrong Kurt, and the fix is that it now refuses to choose', 'Written in the same hour as the entry above, and caught by reading its own output. The register holds KURT LUWINSKI, died 1995, a surname-index entry, and KURT ISRAEL LUWINSKI, 1912 to 1968, who is the man this archive is about. The linker tested exact names first and only fell back to first-plus-last, so the index man matched exactly and won while the real one never got a vote — and WLD 1950/1948, WLD 497/1951 and WLD 2368/1951 were attached to a stranger who outlived Kurt by twenty-seven years. It now pools every candidate before deciding and LINKS NEITHER when more than one person answers to a name, naming both on the page so a human resolves it. A name match is not a person match. This archive has now said that about a census index, a surname-tagged DNA match, three Frederick Castles, eighteen Bermondsey Wears and two Emma Hawkinses — and then written a script that did it anyway. The same check also turned up a DUPLICATE the register\'s own collapse list does not cover: Johannes Antonie Oelofse De Witt is in the source twice, once with a death date and once without.', 'disp'],
  ['And the second guard caught the first one leaking', 'The first build of the new evidence page failed check-matches: a free-text note in the match-derived Castle file identifies a living DNA match holder as somebody\'s grandson, and publishing the file published him. The builder now carries a per-file redaction list and that column never reaches the page. The guard written eight days ago, after three living people were leaked into a build, caught the tool written to fix a different problem on its first run.', 'doc'],
  ['Followed the Pix Road household to the end of all four lives', 'The Pix Road find opened one question worth the day: Louisa Shenton was Henry Robert Wear\'s sister, so a descendant of hers would carry the WEAR half of Derrick\'s ancestry and not the Castle half — the independent test this archive has wanted since it read the segments. THE ANSWER IS NO. Births, surname SHENTON, mother\'s maiden name WEAR: one result, STANLEY W SHENTON, Q1 1924, Hitchin. Deaths, surname Shenton, born 1924: THE SAME CHILD, Q1 1924, Hitchin. Born and died in the same quarter, to a mother of forty-one. And the trap that had caught this archive over Martha Maud was checked for: the district was searched with NO mother\'s name at all, Shenton at Hitchin across twenty-one years, and there is exactly one Shenton birth in it. WILLIAM SHENTON married a Wear at Hitchin in Q3 1918 and died there in Q3 1953. LOUISE S A SHENTON died at Hitchin in Q4 1967, aged 84 — and the initials close it, because her birth is registered as LOUISA SARAH A WEAR, Q1 1883, at KINGSTON, SURREY, which is a place neither of her parents had ever been put before. AND EMMA HULL DIED IN Q2 1944 AT HITCHIN, AGED EIGHTY-FIVE, four years after the grandson she had known as a boy was taken prisoner. Eighty-five years from Aldenham to Letchworth, two husbands buried, and thirty miles.', 'doc'],
  ['Wrote a grandmother over her own mother, by matching on a name', 'The Pix Road finding was applied to the tree by a script that selected people BY NAME. Emma Hawkins the daughter, born 7 February 1859, and Emma Hawkins the mother, born about 1822, are two entries two generations apart with identical names — and the code did not look at the generation. For part of 17 September this archive published that a woman born about 1822 was blind at Letchworth in 1939. The same run appended a sentence to the Giddins household at Norton End saying that THEIR daughter Emma was born on 7 February 1859; their Emma is EMMA GIDDINS, born about 1855 at Norton, a different woman in a different parish who shares only a forename. Both are withdrawn on the pages themselves. A match on a name is not a match on a person — which is the thing this archive says about every census index it touches, said while doing it.', 'disp'],
  ['Caroline James stops being an index line and becomes a household', 'The remarriage had rested on a GRO index entry. The 1921 census settles it from both directions. There is NO Caroline Castle anywhere in Hertfordshire in 1921 — she leaves the surname exactly when the index says she changed it. And there is one Caroline James: RG15/07079, schedule 125, 9 BOSCOMBE PLACE, LETCHWORTH, wife of JAMES JAMES, born at WESTON, Hertfordshire — with ERNEST ALBERT CYRIL CASTLE and FLORENCE MARGARITE HILDA CASTLE in the house, enumerated as his STEP-CHILDREN. That is an enumerator writing down a remarriage. Her age, 56 years 4 months, puts her birthday in February and agrees with the 28th; the year is two out and she is the one who gave it. She had also moved to Letchworth, where her daughter was raising the Wears.', 'doc'],
  ['Nine children born, eight living, and only six have been found', 'The 1911 census at St Mary\'s Cottages, Baldock — RG14/7572, schedule 173 — asks a married woman how many children she has borne. Caroline answers NINE BORN, EIGHT LIVING, ONE DIED. This archive has names for six from the GRO index and a seventh in Martha Maud. So at least two Castle children have never been identified and one of the nine was already dead by 1911. Her own father, JOSEPH WALLIS, is in the house as well, spelt Wallis — a seventh independent instance of the spelling that was in dispute a day ago.', 'doc'],
  ['Excluded the wrong Frederick, with an exact date this time', 'Frederick James Castle, born 1889 at Hitchin to a mother née Wallis, is the only Castle male line that could ever carry a Y test. The Frederick James Castle who died at Hitchin in 1975 is NOT him: the 1939 Register has that man at 17 ORCHARD ROAD, BALDOCK, born 10 APRIL 1887, a vertical driller on aircraft work, married to Ella. And Baldock supplies the warning as well as the problem — a search of the 1921 census returns a SECOND unrelated Castle household at 5 Cemetery Road with nine children and its own Frederick, born about April 1888. Three men of one name in one small town within two years. Our Frederick is in no English census after 1901 and in no death index at all, which is now the open question rather than a solved one.', 'inf'],
  ['Sixty-three and seventy-two Pix Road', 'The 1939 Register was searched for Leonard\'s aunt Louisa Shenton and returned his grandmother. Schedule 305, 63 PIX ROAD, LETCHWORTH: EMMA HULL, born 7 February 1859, widowed, "Incapacitated - Blind"; Louisa Shenton, born 29 November 1882; William Shenton, incapacitated. Emma Hull is EMMA HAWKINS — born at Aldenham, widowed at thirty-one in 1890, remarried to John Hull and widowed again. Henry Robert and Martha Maud had raised their six at 72 PIX ROAD, the same street. The register was taken on 29 September 1939, seven weeks after the last ship sailed, and it finds an eighty-year-old blind woman and the daughter keeping her still in the road the family had left. The record says who was where and nothing about what anybody felt. But it is no longer true that the family simply left.', 'doc'],
  ['Learned that the paywall was not one', 'The estate references looked to be behind a subscription. They are not: every South African estate over the threshold must be advertised as a Form J193 notice in the Government Gazette, which is free and full-text to 2026. The only trick is the name order — modern notices read surname first. Four estates in twenty minutes, all four women born Wear, with exact birth dates, identity numbers and addresses.', 'doc'],
];
---
<Base title="Research log"
  subtitle="What was done, in the order it was done"
  lede="An archive is only as trustworthy as its account of itself. This is the working record — including the leads that went nowhere, which are the ones most worth writing down.">

  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th></th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {log.map(([what, detail, tag]) => (
        <tr>
          <td><strong>{what}</strong></td>
          <td class="small">{detail}</td>
          <td><span class={'tag ' + tag}>{tag === 'doc' ? 'Documented' : tag === 'disp' ? 'Withdrawn' : 'Inferred'}</span></td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>Where the open work stands</h2>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Line of enquiry</th><th>State</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Arolsen Archives</strong> — the International Tracing Service files</td><td>Confirmed to hold <strong>213 Leibholz person records</strong> and 43 document topics. Not yet enumerated.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Sweep all 5,464 DNA matches</strong> for Leibholz and Luwinski</td><td>Six opened in full, three British and three Portuguese; every one reports no shared ancestral surnames. Strong, not exhaustive.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Henrique's South African trail</strong></td><td>A useful negative — <strong>no Halsey records in the Transvaal depot at all</strong>. His 1962 immigration and 2022 death notice still unfound.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Szubin Jewish registers</strong></td><td>Not yet attempted. Where the family predates Berlin.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Reichsanzeiger expatriation lists</strong></td><td>Digitised and full-text searchable. <strong>39,006 people were denaturalised</strong> across 359 lists, 1933–1945. If the Reich stripped Kurt's citizenship he is in them.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>The next fifteen</h2>
  <p>
    Ordered by expected value against effort, not by how interesting they are. <strong>The first five
    need nobody's permission but the applicant's own, and three of them are already located to a
    document number.</strong>
  </p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th></th><th>Action</th><th>Why it is worth doing</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>1</strong></td><td><strong>Order estate 12294/2023</strong></td><td>Henrique's <strong>J294 death notice</strong> asks outright for the full names of the father. The likeliest single answer to question 8</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>2</strong></td><td><strong>Order estate 7584/69</strong> — from the <strong>National Archives</strong>, not the Master <span class="tag disp">Corrected</span></td><td>Kurt's own J294 — what the family said in 1969 about whose son he was. <strong>Corrected the same day:</strong> he died in 1968 and files of that age went to Pretoria long ago. NAAIRS indexes it as <strong>TAB, MHG 7584/69</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>3</strong></td><td>Send the <strong>Porto <em>óbito</em></strong> request</td><td>Portuguese death records name both parents outright. Drafted months ago, never sent</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>4</strong></td><td>Send the <strong>1924 Lourenço Marques birth</strong> request</td><td><strong>1924 falls inside</strong> the Maputo archive's 1865–1934 range, unlike Henrique's 1941. Should give parents <em>and</em> grandparents</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>5</strong></td><td><strong>Henrique's 1968 marriage record</strong></td><td>1968 South African registers <em>do</em> carry parents — unlike the 1948 form, which has no parents column at all</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>6</strong></td><td>The <strong>June 1948 ante-nuptial contract</strong></td><td>Found by re-reading column 10 at full resolution. Executed the month Derrick's parents married, registered in the Johannesburg Deeds Office, and not confined to an eleven-column form</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>7</strong></td><td>The <strong>February 1956 contract</strong></td><td>He did it again with Frances. Same office</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>8</strong></td><td>Write to the <strong>closest British match</strong></td><td>Her common ancestors with Derrick are <strong>his own grandparents</strong>, and her shared ancestral place is <strong>South Africa</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>9</strong></td><td>Write to the <strong>10,715-person tree</strong></td><td>Ask whether a cousin of her father's went to South Africa — and about the grandfather her tree records only as <strong>Unknown</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>10</strong></td><td><strong>Big Y-700</strong></td><td>There are <strong>42 branches below R-S11477</strong>. A Big Y places him on one and opens FamilyTreeDNA's Y match list, which MyHeritage does not have</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>11</strong></td><td><strong>Kurt's naturalisation</strong></td><td>The Union gazette published naturalisation lists, giving birth date and place over an official signature. Bears on question 4 — the <em>Israel</em></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>12</strong></td><td>Finish the <strong>gazette sweep</strong></td><td>Oppenheimer, de Witt, Figueiredo, Salkow narrowed to Johannesburg — and when did Kurt let the hat shop go after 1952?</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>13</strong></td><td><strong>Gertrud's death register</strong>, 27 Feb 1943</td><td>She died in the city, not in a camp, on the day of the <em>Fabrikaktion</em>. The entry gives cause of death</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>14</strong></td><td><strong>Auguste and Jacob's divorce file</strong></td><td>Recorded with no date — and in a Berlin <em>Mischehe</em> the date is the whole meaning of the act</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>15</strong></td><td><strong>Szubin and Hammerstein registers</strong></td><td>Settle whether Georg was Jacob's brother or his cousin. Born two months apart in different provinces, which no mother manages</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Six letters, one bounce, and two bad addresses <span class="tag doc">15 September 2026</span></h3>
    <p>
      Sent: the <strong>Arquivo da Defesa Nacional</strong>; the <strong>Arquivo Histórico de
      Moçambique</strong> twice, once for the recruitment records and once for <strong>Ivone's 1924
      birth certificate</strong> — which, unlike Henrique's 1941 birth, falls inside their range; the
      <strong>Arquivo Histórico Militar</strong>; and the <strong>Master of the High Court</strong> for
      the J294 in estate 12294/2023.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>One bounced, and it was my fault.</strong> The Military Archive's published address,
      <code>ahm@mail.exercito.pt</code>, failed with NXDOMAIN. A DNS lookup shows <code>exercito.pt</code>
      has mail servers and <code>mail.exercito.pt</code> has none — the subdomain has been retired, so the
      address that appears on reference pages everywhere was never going to work for anyone. The resend
      went to <code>ahm@exercito.pt</code> and says so in its first paragraph, so the archive learns its
      own contact is broken.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Then it happened again an hour later.</strong> A summary offered
      <code>MastersJohannesburg@justice.gov.za</code> for the Master's office. That office's own contact
      sheet says, in capitals on its first page, that the address <strong>is not in use</strong>. Reading
      the document instead of the summary also produced the thing that mattered: Johannesburg allocates
      estate files to controllers <strong>by the last digit of the file number</strong>. 122<strong>94</strong>
      ends in 4, so the request went to the named controller for 4 rather than into a general inbox.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The corrected address works.</strong> Within nine minutes the Arquivo Histórico Militar
      acknowledged receipt — <em>“Confirma-se a receção da sua correspondência eletrónica a qual será
      tratada pelos nossos serviços. Enviaremos a informação solicitada, caso exista, com a brevidade
      possível.”</em> So the DNS lookup was right and the address printed on the reference pages is
      simply dead.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      Their reply also corrects a <strong>third</strong> stale fact from a search summary the same
      morning. The summary placed the archive at <em>Largo dos Caminhos de Ferro</em>; the archive says
      it is at <strong>Largo do Outeirinho da Amendoeira</strong>, that its reading room needs no
      appointment, and that it is open <strong>Monday to Friday, 10:00–12:15 and 13:45–16:45</strong>,
      with document requests and reproductions <strong>10:00–12:00 and 13:45–16:00</strong>.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      <strong>Check the MX record before sending, and read the source rather than the summary of it.</strong>
      Two lessons, one morning, and both are written down here because an archive that hides its own
      mistakes is worth less than one that does not.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Nine letters out by the end of the morning</h3>
    <p>
      Added to the six already listed: the <strong>Landesarchiv Berlin</strong>, in German, carrying
      three questions at once — Gertrud's death entry of 27 February 1943 and its cause of death,
      the Leibholz–Luwinski marriage of 5 January 1924 <em>and the date of the divorce</em>, and
      <strong>Kurt's own 1912 birth entry</strong>, which may carry a marginal note of
      <em>Vaterschaftsanerkennung</em>. He was born twelve years before his parents married and under
      his mother's name, so that margin is the paper basis of the whole Leibholz descent.
    </p>
    <p>
      Also sent: the <strong>National Archives in Pretoria</strong> for the J294 in estate
      <strong>TAB, MHG 7584/69</strong>, and the <strong>1ª Conservatória do Registo Civil do
      Porto</strong> for Ivone's death record — the latter asking, since her date of death is unknown,
      whether a search by name in the indexes is possible at all.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      Every address was checked for an MX record before sending, which is how three of the day's five
      candidate addresses turned out to be wrong before they cost anything.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Eleven letters, and only one has ever bounced</strong> — the one sent before the checking
      began. Two archives have acknowledged receipt: the <strong>Arquivo Histórico Militar</strong> in
      Lisbon, and the <strong>Archiwum Państwowe w Bydgoszczy</strong>, which adds a useful warning —
      requests are dealt with in order of arrival, <em>complicated ones can take more than a month</em>,
      and anything sent without a full name is not considered at all. It also asks, dryly, that the
      sender check their spam folder while waiting.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Three of the fifteen will not be done by this archive <span class="tag open">Yours to decide</span></h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Items 6 and 7 — the two ante-nuptial contracts.</strong> A South African deeds registry
      <em>“may not give out information acting on a letter or a telephone call.”</em> Copies require
      attendance in person, or a commercial search service that charges for it. So this is not a letter
      anybody can write; it needs a person in South Africa or a paid order, and the archive records that
      rather than pretending otherwise.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Item 5 — Henrique's 1968 marriage record.</strong> Searched on both the groom and the
      bride; FamilySearch's South African marriage collections are patchy after the mid-1960s and it is
      not indexed. It may simply arrive inside the estate file already requested, which is the cheaper
      way to the same fact.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Items 8 and 9 — the two letters to living DNA matches.</strong> Both are written and
      neither has been sent. Everything else this archive has posted went to an institution; these go to
      two private people who did not ask to be contacted, about a finding that touches their family as
      well as this one. They are drafted to open with a narrow question rather than with
      non-paternity, to ask only about the dead, and to say plainly that the DNA and the paperwork
      disagree — so that nobody feels drawn in under false pretences. <strong>And Derrick should
      probably be told before a stranger is.</strong> That is not the archive's call, and it is why
      they are sitting still.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      <strong>Item 10 — Big Y-700 — is a purchase</strong>, and this archive does not spend other
      people's money.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Parked, and why</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Arolsen Archives.</strong> 213 Leibholz person records are confirmed to exist. The
      declaration of consent carries a criminal-liability clause, and this archive will not tick it on
      anybody's behalf. It needs the family's own signature.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>And one thing that will not appear here at all.</strong> The archive has been told that
      Derrick served on the Mozambique border in the 1970s. He is living; this archive
      <a href={base + '/derrick/'}>names him and publishes nothing else about him</a>, and that includes
      his service. The single fact such a file could hold that <em>would</em> be publishable is a
      next-of-kin address for <strong>Ivone in the 1970s</strong> — and nobody knows where she was
      between the Salkow marriage in 1953 and her death at Porto. But it is his record, it needs his own
      request, and asking him would be faster than asking Pretoria.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Leads closed, with reasons</h2>
  <p>
    These cost time and produced nothing, which is exactly why they are written down — so that
    nobody spends the same time discovering the same nothing.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>FamilySearch for Mozambique.</strong> The catalogue holds 51 items for the country and not one is a vital record. The search cannot succeed.</li>
    <li><strong>Porto passport registers.</strong> The surviving series runs 1951–1991, is described at series level only, and is not digitised. Half a century too late.</li>
    <li><strong>MyHeritage record collections for Ivone.</strong> Nine results, eight Brazilian and one Floridian. No Portuguese or colonial civil registration in them at all.</li>
    <li><strong>Augusto Pereira de Figueiredo</strong> is a candidate and probably a coincidence: no Figueiredo–Mota connection exists anywhere in Aveiro, and his brother emigrated to Brazil like most of that district.</li>
    <li><strong>Halsey in the South African national archives.</strong> Forty documents, not one from the Transvaal depot. Whoever that family was in Johannesburg, they left no estate file in the index.</li>
    <li><strong>Leibholz in the South African gazettes: zero</strong>, across all 54,313 issues — matching the NAAIRS result. The name never entered South African public record at all.</li>
    <li><strong>Colonial Mozambican gazettes</strong> are not held by Gazettes.Africa; its Mozambique run begins at independence in 1975. And <strong>Projecto GERMIL</strong>, the Portuguese military master books, stops in <strong>1908</strong> — thirty years too early for Henrique.</li>
    <li><strong>The 331,829 new Mozambique images at the Lisbon overseas archive.</strong> They are described at file and series level only — <strong>not searchable by personal name</strong>. They exist; they cannot be queried for Ivone. They would have to be read.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Sibling archives</h2>
  <p>
    Same method, same author. <strong>The Defranceski</strong> (Istria), <strong>The Lerena</strong>
    (Rosario, and the hinge into this one), <strong>The Falco</strong> (Arienzo). The Lerena archive
    recorded this archive's subject under a wrong given name and a wrong year; it has been corrected to
    <strong>Lynn Derrick Luwinski</strong>, and the two now point at each other.
  </p>
